"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,361\nThe Learning Brain. Series 2: Memory\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,416\nEpisode 11\n\n3\n00:00:07,496 --> 00:00:09,952\nRetrieval rewrites memory\n\n4\n00:00:12,752 --> 00:00:15,270\nStudents read a short science passage.\n\n5\n00:00:15,545 --> 00:00:16,909\nSome then read it again.\n\n6\n00:00:17,293 --> 00:00:18,711\nOthers tried to recall it,\n\n7\n00:00:18,731 --> 00:00:19,815\nwith no feedback.\n\n8\n00:00:20,252 --> 00:00:22,756\nFive minutes later, rereading looked better.\n\n9\n00:00:23,188 --> 00:00:24,869\nBut two days and a week later,\n\n10\n00:00:24,902 --> 00:00:27,520\nthe students who had recalled it remembered more.\n\n11\n00:00:27,690 --> 00:00:29,823\nRetrieval is not a simple readout.\n\n12\n00:00:30,275 --> 00:00:32,283\nEach time a memory is pulled up,\n\n13\n00:00:32,529 --> 00:00:34,491\nit is rebuilt and stored again,\n\n14\n00:00:34,609 --> 00:00:37,156\nstrengthened along the path that was used.\n\n15\n00:00:37,497 --> 00:00:39,829\nRereading lets the page do the work.\n\n16\n00:00:40,188 --> 00:00:41,960\nRecall makes the brain do it.\n\n17\n00:00:42,130 --> 00:00:45,577\nA meta-analysis of two hundred and seventy two effects,\n\n18\n00:00:45,724 --> 00:00:47,938\nfrom about fifteen thousand people,\n\n19\n00:00:48,300 --> 00:00:51,006\nfound that practice tests beat restudying,\n\n20\n00:00:51,026 --> 00:00:52,903\nin labs and in classrooms.\n\n21\n00:00:53,302 --> 00:00:55,132\nThey helped even without feedback.\n\n22\n00:00:55,302 --> 00:00:58,237\nRetrieval also reshapes what is not retrieved.\n\n23\n00:00:58,839 --> 00:01:02,392\nWhen people practised recalling some fruits from a studied list,\n\n24\n00:01:02,729 --> 00:01:09,338\nthey later recalled the unpractised fruits worse than items from categories they had not practised at all.\n\n25\n00:01:09,508 --> 00:01:12,008\nAnd retrieval can be steered from outside.\n\n26\n00:01:12,391 --> 00:01:14,742\nPeople watched films of car crashes.\n\n27\n00:01:15,074 --> 00:01:18,277\nAsked how fast the cars were going when they smashed,\n\n28\n00:01:18,315 --> 00:01:21,076\nrather than hit, they gave higher estimates.\n\n29\n00:01:21,463 --> 00:01:26,057\nA week later, more of them reported seeing broken glass that was never there.\n\n30\n00:01:26,227 --> 00:01:27,023\nA caution.\n\n31\n00:01:27,467 --> 00:01:31,474\nTesting helps most when students can retrieve at least some of the answer,\n\n32\n00:01:31,736 --> 00:01:33,415\nand when errors are corrected.\n\n33\n00:01:33,871 --> 00:01:38,234\nAnd a leading question can plant a mistake as easily as it checks knowledge.\n\n34\n00:01:38,404 --> 00:01:39,211\nFor teaching.\n\n35\n00:01:39,629 --> 00:01:43,413\nUse frequent, low stakes quizzes instead of extra review.\n\n36\n00:01:43,821 --> 00:01:46,835\nAsk students to recall before showing the answer,\n\n37\n00:01:46,908 --> 00:01:48,077\nthen give feedback.\n\n38\n00:01:48,559 --> 00:01:50,162\nQuiz across the whole topic,\n\n39\n00:01:50,182 --> 00:01:52,193\nnot just a few favourite points.\n\n40\n00:01:52,448 --> 00:01:54,244\nAnd word questions neutrally.\n\n41\n00:01:54,414 --> 00:01:56,528\nRemembering is not reading a record.\n\n42\n00:01:56,876 --> 00:01:58,381\nEvery time we recall,\n\n43\n00:01:58,531 --> 00:02:00,093\nwe write the memory again.\n\n"
